#
# A `NOT IN` clause that fails as a `when` condition, which is the shape the notice must NOT go out on.
#
# The condition fails -- element-wise `1` is in `[1, 3]` -- and a failing condition is not a failing file.
# `eval_conjunction_clauses` counts the FAIL, `eval_rule` maps every non-PASS condition to SKIP, and the
# body is dropped. So `Encrypted == true`, which this template violates, never runs and the file exits 0
# with an empty report.
#
# That silence reads like a reason to warn, and a previous revision did. It is not. The notice says a
# future release fails closed where this one passes, and this condition does not pass, so the change moves
# nothing here: `!=` already fails closed, and a condition that reaches `NotComparable` skips its rule at
# exit 0 exactly as this one does. Warning would also require saying something false. The body written for
# it claimed "Nothing in the report says so", and the report names this very clause whenever another clause
# in the same rule fails -- add `Encrypted == false` beside the block and stdout prints the gate's own
# comparison at exit 19.
#
# So this fixture pins an absence, and the notice's true positives are pinned on
# `vacuous_and_incomparable_clauses.guard`, where the clause passes.
#
rule ports_not_denied when Resources.A.Properties.Ports NOT IN [1, 3] {
    Resources.A.Properties.Encrypted == true
}
